  "worked_solution": "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{\u2032}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)",
